---
description: |
  Agentmetry events → OCSF.

  Reads a structured Agentmetry event, as its webhook sink posts it or as one
  line of its trail holds it, and builds the corresponding OCSF event in the
  second field:

  ```tql
  agentmetry::ocsf::map agentmetry, ocsf
  ```

  The mapping picks the event class from the action type and, for tool calls,
  from the tool:

  | Agentmetry event                                                   | OCSF class                  |
  | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------- |
  | `tool_called`, `approval_request`, `approval_response`, shell tool | Process Activity (1007)     |
  | the same, file tool with a path                                    | File System Activity (1001) |
  | the same, any other tool                                           | API Activity (6003)         |
  | `detection`                                                        | Detection Finding (2004)    |
  | `mcp_schema` with outcome `changed`                                | Detection Finding (2004)    |

  A `null` event, a record without Agentmetry's `action` section, and every
  other action type, such as `session_start`, a configuration change, or an
  unchanged MCP listing, become an OCSF Base Event.
  A supplied `timestamp_utc` becomes `time` even when the action is missing.
  When the timestamp is absent, `time` remains null.

  The operator consumes the source event: what it maps becomes OCSF, and what it
  cannot map becomes `unmapped` inside the OCSF event. The operator does not
  preserve the original log record.

  ## Semantics

  - Agentmetry names the agent by its harness, such as `claude` or `cursor`, and
    groups one session under `correlation_id`. The mapping adds the AI
    Operation profile and makes the session the `ai_agent.instance_uid` and the
    `metadata.correlation_uid`.
  - Model IDs, model providers, and optional account claims stay under
    `unmapped.model` and `unmapped.account`. A model provider can be an app-name
    fallback such as `codex`, so it does not populate `cloud.provider`. Account
    claims do not replace the operator identity in `actor.user`.
  - Agentmetry's standalone hook hashes tool arguments by default, so a file
    tool call without a captured path has no file to report. It becomes API
    Activity with the tool as the operation.
  - Shell calls preserve the recorded command in `process.cmd_line`, but
    Agentmetry does not report the launched process's identity. A Claude Code
    call carries the harness's tool-use ID, which becomes `process.uid`. For
    other shell calls, OCSF validation warns that `process` lacks `pid`, `uid`,
    or `cpid`. Approval requests and denied calls do not establish that a
    process ran.
  - A tool call that a policy denied gets the Security Control profile with
    action Denied and disposition Blocked. A person's decision on an approval
    request is Approved or Rejected. An inferred response, from a later tool
    call or a session ending with the request open, has Unknown disposition,
    and the inference reason is the status detail.
  - The Claude Code tool-use ID becomes `process.uid` of a shell call and
    `api.request.uid` of an API call. A file call keeps it under
    `unmapped.tool.invocation`, together with the phase, hook event, and
    provenance of every observation. The ID relates observations only within
    one session from one recorder, so join on it together with
    `metadata.correlation_uid`. A result's reported status becomes
    `status_code` and its reported execution time becomes `duration`. Both are
    what the harness reported, not a verified process exit. A request has
    neither. Claude Code reports failed calls only through the
    `PostToolUseFailure` hook, which existing installations receive after they
    refresh their Agentmetry hooks.
  - A detection is a sequence rule that matched across the calls of one
    session. The rule becomes the analytic, the matched calls become
    `finding_info.related_events`, and the ATT&CK identifiers become `attacks`.
    Evidence includes the identified acting user and, when named, the agent.
    Without an identified user, the agent stays under `unmapped.agent` because
    OCSF 1.9 does not accept an AI agent alone as evidence.
  - The tool calls in `detection.artifacts` become evidence with their event
    ID and tool, plus the file path or URL when Agentmetry captured one. These
    calls can stem from other sessions, so they do not inherit the triggering
    actor. Their outcomes stay under `unmapped`, so evidence of a denied call
    shows an attempt, not an execution.
  - An `mcp_schema` event with outcome `changed` says that the `tools/list` of a
    known MCP server changed, the shape of an MCP rug pull. Agentmetry assigns
    it no severity.
  - MITRE ATLAS identifiers, the per-call ATT&CK classification of a tool, DLP
    matches, and the hash-chain fields of a trail line have no OCSF attribute
    and stay under `unmapped`. The chain hashes cover the Agentmetry event, not
    the OCSF event, so they cannot become a Record Integrity attestation.
args:
  positional:
    - name: agentmetry
      type: field
      description: The structured Agentmetry event to read.
    - name: ocsf
      type: field
      description: The OCSF event to build.
---

$ocsf = {
  metadata: {
    version: "1.9.0",
    product: {
      name: "Agentmetry",
      vendor_name: "Agentmetry",
    },
    profiles: ["host"],
  },
  severity_id: 1,
}

if $agentmetry.event? != null and $agentmetry.trail? != null {
  $agentmetry = {...move $agentmetry.event, trail: move $agentmetry.trail}
}

if $agentmetry.action?.type? != null {
  // Agentmetry writes empty strings for what does not apply, such as the
  // reason of a tool call that succeeded.
  replace $agentmetry, what="", with=null
  agentmetry::ocsf::common $agentmetry, $ocsf
}

// Preserve source time even when the action is missing. Convert timestamps
// from record literals as well as timestamps inferred by a JSON reader.
// Agentmetry writes microseconds. Rounding to them undoes the one-nanosecond
// error with which Tenzir 6.23 and 7.0.0-rc.6 parse some fractions; remove it
// once every supported version parses times exactly (Linear TNZ-2181).
$ocsf.time = time(move $agentmetry.timestamp_utc?).round(1us)

match $ocsf.metadata.event_code? {
  "tool_called" | "approval_request" | "approval_response" => {
    agentmetry::ocsf::tool $agentmetry, $ocsf
  }
  "detection" => {
    agentmetry::ocsf::events::detection $agentmetry, $ocsf
  }
  "mcp_schema" if $agentmetry.action.outcome == "changed" => {
    agentmetry::ocsf::events::mcp_schema_changed $agentmetry, $ocsf
  }
  _ => {
    @name = "ocsf.base_event"
    $ocsf.category_uid = 0
    $ocsf.class_uid = 0
    $ocsf.activity_id = 0
    $ocsf.message = move $agentmetry.action?.reason?
  }
}

drop $agentmetry.correlation_id?
$ocsf.type_uid = $ocsf.class_uid * 100 + $ocsf.activity_id
if $ocsf.action_id? != null {
  $ocsf.metadata.profiles = [...$ocsf.metadata.profiles, "security_control"]
}
$ocsf = {...$ocsf, unmapped: move $agentmetry}
